data.content:
This is a well-documented C# 14 breaking change. Here's what's happening:

## Root Cause

C# 14 introduces new **span conversions**. When you call `.Contains()` on an `int[]`, the compiler now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in expression trees**, so this throws at runtime (an `InvalidOperationException` or `NotSupportedException` when the expression tree is compiled/interpreted).

## Fix

Force the compiler to resolve back to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: Call Enumerable.Contains explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevent the span overload from being selected and keep the call representable in an expression tree.
